## HTTP/3 Uses QUIC over UDP (Not TCP)

HTTP/3 abandoned TCP entirely and instead runs on **QUIC** (Quick UDP Internet Connections), a transport protocol built on top of **UDP**. This is the most significant transport-layer change in HTTP's history. [1](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716) [2](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716)

### Why They Switched

The switch happened because TCP's fundamental design created hard limits that HTTP/2 could not overcome, even with multiplexing:

- **Head-of-Line Blocking:** HTTP/2 runs multiple streams over a single TCP connection. TCP guarantees in-order byte delivery, so if one packet is lost, the kernel buffers *all* subsequent packets across *every* stream until the missing packet is retransmitted. One lost packet stalls every download. QUIC solves this by giving each stream independent packet ordering—loss on one stream only blocks that stream. [2](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716) [3](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716)

- **Slow Connection Setup:** TCP requires a three-way handshake (1 RTT) followed by a TLS handshake (1–2 RTTs), totaling 2–3 round trips before any data flows. QUIC merges the transport and cryptographic handshake into **1 RTT** for new connections, and supports **0-RTT** resumption for returning visitors—allowing data in the very first packet. [1](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716) [2](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716)

- **Connection Breaks on IP Changes:** TCP identifies a connection by source/destination IP and port. Switch from Wi-Fi to 4G? The connection drops and must be re-established. QUIC uses a stable **Connection ID**, so the session survives network changes and IP address changes seamlessly. [3](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716)

- **TCP Ossification:** TCP lives inside operating system kernels and network middleboxes. Changing TCP behavior requires OS updates and hardware upgrades, taking years or decades. QUIC runs entirely in **userspace** (inside browsers and servers), so fixes and improvements deploy at application speed—weeks, not years. [2](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716) [4](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716)

- **Mandatory Security:** While HTTP/2 made TLS optional in principle, QUIC bakes **TLS 1.3** into the transport itself. Encryption is mandatory, not bolted-on, and nearly every byte after the first packet is encrypted. [2](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716) [3](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716)

### The Bottom Line

HTTP/3 uses **QUIC over UDP** because TCP's in-order delivery and kernel-level implementation made it impossible to solve head-of-line blocking, reduce handshake latency, or adapt quickly to modern mobile networks. By rebuilding transport logic in userspace on top of UDP, QUIC keeps the reliability where it matters (per-stream) while eliminating the architectural constraints that held HTTP/2 back. [2](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716) [4](#c0a6ca12-d423-435c-b76f-77da26d98716)

